1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,420
Have you ever watched one of the many TV shows set during the apocalypse - a dystopian future where life as we know it is over and people are only focussed on survival?
你是否看过那些以末日为背景的电视剧——在反乌托邦的未来，我们熟知的生活已然终结，人们一心只想着活下去？

2
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:18,180
If it’s not zombies, it’s viruses or natural disasters.
故事的导火索不是僵尸来袭，就是病毒肆虐或自然灾害爆发。

3
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:26,960
One thing that seems to be a quintessential part of these series is survivors searching for food and resources.
这类剧集的一个典型特征，就是幸存者四处搜寻食物和资源。

4
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:31,620
Is that why people stockpile food when things go bad?
难道这就是世道艰难时人们会囤积食物的原因吗？

5
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,450
What is stockpiling?
什么是囤积？

6
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:43,700
Quite simply it’s when people accumulate a substantial number of goods, for example food or water.
很简单，就是人们大量囤积物资，比如食物或水。

7
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:56,260
According to psychologist Judy Rosenberg, stockpiling perishable goods like eggs and milk is a positive sign that people believe a crisis will be over soon.
心理学家朱迪·罗森伯格表示，囤积鸡蛋、牛奶这类易腐物资，是人们相信危机很快就会过去的积极信号。

8
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:03,210
When people stockpile tinned goods, it can be a sign that people fear that the crisis will endure.
而如果囤积的是罐装食品，则可能意味着人们担心危机将会持续很长时间。

9
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:05,460
But why do we do it?
但我们为什么会这么做呢？

10
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:13,130
Consumer psychologist Paul Marsden puts it down to three factors that relate to retail therapy.
消费心理学家保罗·马斯登将其归结为与购物疗法相关的三个因素。

11
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:20,130
Firstly, ‘autonomy’ – the idea the people feel the need to be in control.
第一是“自主掌控感”——人们会觉得自己需要掌控局面。

12
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:31,100
Secondly, ‘relatedness’ – panic shopping helps people to feel connected to the community or other shoppers who are doing the same.
第二是“归属感”——恐慌性购物会让人们觉得自己和社区里的人，或是和其他同样在抢购的人产生了联结。

13
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:40,330
Lastly, ‘competence’ - people want to feel like they are ‘smart shoppers’ and doing the right thing.
第三是“价值感”——人们希望自己能成为“精明的购物者”，做的事情是正确的。

14
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:52,760
Sander Van der Lindon, an assistant professor of social psychology at Cambridge University, said that a phenomenon known as ‘fear contagion’ can take hold.
剑桥大学社会心理学助理教授桑德·范德林登表示，一种名为“恐惧传染”的现象可能会随之蔓延。

15
00:01:52,760 --> 00:02:00,250
People’s decision making is hampered when stressed, so they follow the actions of other people.
人在压力之下，决策能力会受到影响，因此会下意识模仿他人的行为。

16
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:04,040
If they are panic buying, you will too.
如果别人都在恐慌性抢购，你也会跟着这么做。

17
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:08,500
So, can you stop people from stockpiling?
那么，有办法阻止人们囤积物资吗？

18
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:20,050
Well, supermarkets can ration the amount of products consumers purchase, and governments can try to reassure people that there is no need to panic.
超市可以对消费者的购买量进行定量供应，政府也可以努力安抚民众，让大家不必恐慌。

19
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:26,920
However, it seems that it goes against our basic psychology to try and stop people from panic buying.
但事实似乎是，阻止人们进行恐慌性购物，是违背人类基本心理的。

20
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:41,380
So, the next time a virus, natural disaster or zombie apocalypse starts, remember that panic buying is probably a given, thanks to our need for retail therapy to manage our emotional state.
所以下次再遇到病毒蔓延、自然灾害或是僵尸末日这样的情况时，要记住恐慌性购物或许是必然会发生的——毕竟我们需要靠购物疗愈来调节自己的情绪状态。
